window.REVIEW_DETAILS["ny_main-9b916f31a7713596"]={"key":"ny_main-9b916f31a7713596","group":"ny_main","uid":"9b916f31a7713596","rank":44,"title":"wrenfield emergency access doctrinal standard","occupation":"lawyers","archetype":"authored_doc","subbatch":"corpus_main","prompt":"You are Tadeo Zwerling, a lawyer at Ashgrove Chambers advising Wrenfield Health Network. Bianca Moravec has found duplicate access-log entries, malformed entity codes, and inconsistent uses of “emergency,” “urgent,” and “break-glass” in material shared across Wrenfield and its parent, Marchbank Holdings; those records do not sit comfortably with the frozen external control snapshot. Wrenfield is moving from the legacy arrangement to a single enterprise emergency-access standard, but the old practice will remain in use during the transition and the target state is expected shortly afterwards. Laila Kaczmarek needs a revised cross-functional control standard for approval and publication by 5 p.m. tomorrow. The available review window is 48 hours, and the team cannot reconstruct the entire historical log or perform a patient-by-patient review before that deadline, so the document must handle that constraint in a defensible way rather than pretending it does not exist. A particularly difficult case involves an emergency in which the patient’s identity is central to understanding the event: the record needs a complete justification, but it should not expose more patient information than is necessary. Using `frozen_emergency_access_control_snapshot.pdf` as the sole evidence source, write `revised_emergency_access_control_standard.docx` and place it at the top level of the working directory. The document should state, in a form that clinical, privacy, security, audit, and governance readers can use, which factual claims and control labels the frozen evidence supports, which claims it cannot establish, and how the competing completeness and data-minimization requirements are expressed for the edge case. It must distinguish genuine emergency access from staff using break-glass access as a convenient substitute for ordinary approval, while remaining useful during the legacy-to-enterprise transition and describing the intended end state. Treat the snapshot’s as-of date as material. Keep the client-ready document to no more than six pages, avoid unnecessary patient detail, and present the facts and standard without recommending an operational action, implementation plan, or resource decision to Wrenfield or Marchbank.\n\n---\n\nThe files referred to above are in `references/`:\n\n- `references/frozen_emergency_access_control_snapshot.pdf` — The sole frozen external evidence source for the emergency-access control labels, supported and unsupported claims, audit observations, privacy requirement, and transition context used in the revised standard.\n\nWrite your finished work to the **top level of the current working directory**, using exactly these filenames:\n\n- `revised_emergency_access_control_standard.docx` — A client-ready revised emergency-access control standard for Wrenfield Health Network and the Marchbank Holdings transition, grounded in the supplied frozen snapshot. (Use exactly this filename and write the file to the top level of the working directory.; The rendered document must not exceed six pages.; State the snapshot as-of date and distinguish supported evidence from claims the evidence cannot establish.; Address the emergency-versus-convenience distinction, the justification and data-minimization edge case, duplicate and malformed log evidence, and both the transition period and intended end state.; Do not include an operational recommendation, implementation plan, staffing or resource recommendation, or patient-identifying detail not needed for the standard.)\n\nNothing outside those filenames is collected. A correct file written anywhere else, or under any other name, scores zero.\n","inputs":[{"name":"package/public/reference_files/frozen_emergency_access_control_snapshot.pdf","bytes":7462,"extension":".pdf","url":"assets/92e04e4f4e5f44ee4d79511c4a6b1077a739dd6cc4e19900a31ca5b780cbd4b3.pdf","sha256":"92e04e4f4e5f44ee4d79511c4a6b1077a739dd6cc4e19900a31ca5b780cbd4b3","preview":{"kind":"pdf","text":"                                                                EXTERNAL CONTROL OFFICE\n\n\n                         Frozen Emergency Access Control Snapshot\n                                        Wrenfield Health Network / Marchbank Holdings enterprise context\n\n\n         FROZEN EVIDENCE\n                                                As of 2025-06-30 | External control-office snapshot\n         POSITION\n\n\n\n Scope of this snapshot\n This frozen extract records the control vocabulary and evidence position used for enterprise emergency or break-glass access to patient\n and clinical records. It is a temporal record as at the stated date, not a reconstruction of all historical activity.\n\n Control vocabulary\n   Control          Frozen control statement\n\n   EAC-01           Emergency access is limited to an immediate patient-safety or continuity-of-care risk where ordinary authorization is unavailable\n                    or delay through it would create harmful delay.\n\n   EAC-02           The event record must contain the actor, event time, affected patient/record or entity reference, reason for access, and outcome\n                    or review reference sufficient to assess the event.\n\n   EAC-03           Emergency-access activity is subject to retrospective review; review is an accountability control and is not original authorization.\n\n   DAT-02           Audit and justification records use the minimum necessary patient identifier and related detail for the purpose of understanding\n                    and assessing the event.\n\n   AUD-04           Audit records use a canonical entity-code concept and preserve event integrity; malformed or unresolved codes are evidence\n                    limitations, not grounds for silent inference.\n\n\n\n Observed log excerpt\n Selected rows retained in the frozen material. References are non-identifying system references.\n  Row      Event ref    Actor ref    Event time (UTC)        Record ref    Recorded         Entity code        Review\n                                                                           label                               ref\n\n  3412     EV-22016     BRK-701      2025-06-28 02:14:08 REC-8J4Q          break-glass      WHN-ED-04          RR-861\n\n  3413     EV-22016     BRK-701      2025-06-28 02:14:08 REC-8J4Q          break-glass      WHN-ED-04          RR-861\n\n  3471     EV-22019     CLN-164      2025-06-28 06:43:11 REC-2Y7M          Urgent           MBH-CV-??          RR-865\n\n  3518     EV-22022     IMR-052      2025-06-28 11:05:42 REC-0K1T          Emergency        WNH_ER4            RR-869\n\n  3530     EV-22023     BRK-702      2025-06-28 13:35:09 REC-4P6D          Break Glass                         RR-873\n\n  3557     EV-22027     SUP-018      2025-06-28 16:51:26 REC-7C3L          urgent           MBH-W-1?7          RR-876\n\n\n\n\nExternal Control Office | Frozen control snapshot | As of 2025-06-30                                                                                       Page 1\n\f Observed evidence and its limits\n The frozen material contains duplicate log representation of the same underlying access event: the matched rows share the event\n reference, actor, event time, record reference, label, entity code, and review reference. It also contains entity-code values that are\n malformed or cannot be resolved to the canonical entity-code concept. Emergency-related labels are inconsistent across the records,\n including emergency, urgent, and break-glass variants.\n These observations support that the records and stated data-quality defects exist. They do not establish that every emergency-related label\n denotes a justified emergency or that every such event was properly authorized. A recorded event is distinct from proof that the emergency\n criterion or authorization requirement was satisfied. An absent, malformed, or unresolved entity code does not establish that access was\n unauthorized or that no emergency existed.\n Historical labels remain evidence of what was recorded; they are not automatically conforming target-state labels. The snapshot does not\n establish universal compliance, universal non-compliance, or a conclusion beyond the observed material.\n\n\n Justification and minimum-necessary detail\n A complete justification can require a patient identity or record reference where that identity is central to understanding the emergency.\n DAT-02 limits the record to the minimum identifier and related context necessary to understand and assess the event. This snapshot does\n not support either an absolute rule to omit all identifiers or an unrestricted clinical narrative.\n\n Transition and review boundary\n A legacy logging and label arrangement remains during migration to one enterprise standard. The target state uses controlled\n emergency-access language, canonical entity-code treatment, the EAC-02 event-context fields, and retrospective review. Historical\n artifacts do not automatically demonstrate target-state conformity, and this snapshot does not prescribe an implementation sequence.\n The recorded review window is 48 hours. The material does not include a complete historical-log reconstruction or patient-by-patient\n validation; conclusions must remain bounded to the frozen evidence position as of 2025-06-30.\n\n Claim-status register\n   Supported by the snapshot                                              Not established by the snapshot\n\n   Named controls and their stated meanings; duplicate representation,    That every emergency, urgent, or break-glass record was a genuine\n   malformed or unresolved codes, inconsistent emergency-related          emergency, authorized, or compliant; that a code defect proves\n   labels; event context, minimum-necessary identifiers, and              unauthorized access or no emergency; that review cures an unjustified\n   retrospective review.                                                  original access.\n\n   Historical labels evidence what was recorded; the legacy arrangement   That minimization permits removal of essential event context; that\n   remains during transition.                                             completeness permits unrestricted patient detail; or that the target\n                                                                          state already applies uniformly to legacy records.\n\n\n\n\nExternal Control Office | Frozen control snapshot | As of 2025-06-30                                                                              Page 2\n\f","truncated":false,"note":"PDF 文字提取；可打开原文件查看排版。"}}],"gold":[{"name":"package/private/gold/revised_emergency_access_control_standard.docx","bytes":43036,"extension":".docx","url":"assets/95474002985d834d7382076a5d4196f00b44fc8d873029254e371098de7de1d4.docx","sha256":"95474002985d834d7382076a5d4196f00b44fc8d873029254e371098de7de1d4","preview":{"kind":"text","text":"Revised Emergency-Access Control Standard\nWrenfield Health Network | Marchbank Holdings transition\nInstrument\nEvidence anchor\nCurrent status\nEnterprise control standard\nFrozen snapshot as of 30 June 2025\nTransitional standard; intended enterprise end state stated below\n1. Purpose, scope, and evidence position\nThis standard states the enterprise control boundary for emergency or break-glass access to patient and clinical records. It applies to the workforce and support actors who initiate, facilitate, record, review, or oversee that access, including clinical, privacy, security, audit, and governance functions. It is not confined to one department and does not make an individual finding about any person or event.\nEvidence anchor. The evidence position is anchored solely to the External Control Office Frozen Emergency Access Control Snapshot as of 30 June 2025. Source observations are stated as observations; the provisions headed as standards are normative control requirements. The snapshot is material only as of that date and is not treated as a reconstruction of later or unreviewed history.\nFACTUAL OBSERVATION\nThe snapshot supports the existence of specified records, stated control labels, duplicate representation, malformed or unresolved codes, and inconsistent emergency-related labels.\nNORMATIVE STATUS\nThis standard supplies a single control expression for the transition. A record’s label or presence in a log is not, by itself, proof that it met that expression.\n\nConstrained evidence-status fallback. The source records a 48-hour review window and no complete historical-log reconstruction or patient-by-patient validation. During that bounded period, an item that cannot be reconciled to the available event and code evidence remains unresolved for evidence purposes: it is neither deemed compliant nor deemed unauthorized solely from a label, missing value, or malformed value. That fallback preserves uncertainty; it is not a claim that full reconciliation or patient-by-patient validation has occurred.\n2. Snapshot-supported control register and emergency boundary\nThe following labels and meanings are supported by the frozen snapshot. Their inclusion preserves the source vocabulary; it does not convert every historical use of a label into proof of conformity.\nLabel\nSnapshot-supported control meaning\nEAC-01\nEmergency access is limited to an immediate patient-safety or continuity-of-care risk where ordinary authorization is unavailable or delay through it would create harmful delay.\nEAC-02\nThe event record must contain the actor, event time, affected patient/record or entity reference, reason for access, and outcome or review reference sufficient to assess the event.\nEAC-03\nEmergency-access activity is subject to retrospective review; review is an accountability control and is not original authorization.\nDAT-02\nAudit and justification records use the minimum necessary patient identifier and related detail for the purpose of understanding and assessing the event.\nAUD-04\nAudit records use a canonical entity-code concept and preserve event integrity; malformed or unresolved codes are evidence limitations, not grounds for silent inference.\nEmergency-access criterion. Emergency access is limited to an immediate patient-safety or continuity-of-care risk where ordinary authorization is unavailable, or delay through the ordinary path would create harmful delay. Both the immediate risk and the unavailable-or-harmful-delay ordinary-authorization condition are required. The terms emergency, urgent, and break-glass are recorded labels, not independent proof that this criterion was met.\nBreak-glass boundary. Break-glass access is not a convenient substitute for ordinary approval where the situation is planned, routine, or capable of tolerating the ordinary authorization path. A later label, explanation, or retrospective review cannot retroactively turn a non-emergency access into a genuine emergency.\nRecorded event boundary. A log row establishes only that an access event or its representation was recorded. The separate questions of necessity, emergency status, authorization, and compliance require the applicable contemporaneous event context; they are not resolved by a record’s appearance or label alone.\n3. Event justification and the identity-central case\nMinimum event record. For each emergency-access event, the justification record must contain the actor, event time, affected patient/record or entity reference, reason for access, and outcome or review reference sufficient to assess the event. A complete justification means enough contemporaneous context to assess necessity and link the access to the affected record. It does not demand an unrestricted clinical narrative.\nCOMPLETENESS\nThe record retains the context essential to identify and understand the emergency event, assess necessity, and link the event to the affected record.\nDATA MINIMIZATION\nThe record contains only the minimum necessary patient identifier and related detail needed for that assessment. Completeness does not authorize unrelated detail.\n\nIdentity-central edge case. Where the patient’s identity is central to understanding the emergency, the record must include only the minimum patient identifier or record reference necessary to establish that context and must state why that identifier is needed for the event. This is the deliberate balance between justification completeness and data minimization.\nPaired limits. Data minimization does not permit omission of information essential to identify or understand the emergency event. Conversely, a complete justification does not authorize extraneous patient identifiers or clinical detail unrelated to the access event. Neither an absolute omission rule nor an unrestricted narrative is supported by the snapshot.\n4. Event integrity, code status, labels, and review\nDuplicate representation. Where shared event attributes establish that multiple log rows represent the same underlying access event, they are treated as one event with duplicate representations and are not counted as separate substantive access events. Similar-looking rows are not collapsed merely because a timestamp, label, or other single attribute resembles another row; genuinely distinct access events remain distinct.\nEntity-code status. The canonical entity-code concept is the controlled requirement under AUD-04. A legacy, absent, malformed, or unresolved code is not presented as a conforming canonical identifier and remains visibly unresolved as an evidence limitation. It is not silently repaired from an emergency-related label, and its defect does not by itself prove that access was unauthorized or that no emergency existed.\nLabel status. Inconsistent uses of emergency, urgent, and break-glass are a control-language and evidence-consistency issue. No historical label is selected as authoritative merely because it sounds strongest. Controlled emergency-access language and canonical entity-code mappings are target-state vocabulary requirements; historical labels remain evidence of what was recorded, not proof of target-state conformity.\nRetrospective review. Emergency-access activity is subject to retrospective review. Review is an accountability control that may assess the event record and justification; it is not original authorization and does not cure an access that was unjustified when made.\n5. Transition status and intended enterprise treatment\nTransition status. The legacy logging and label arrangement remains in use during the transition. Its historical records and labels may show what was recorded and may be evaluated against this standard’s evidence boundaries, but they do not automatically satisfy the enterprise target standard. This standard does not imply that the target-state treatment is already fully in force in every legacy record or system.\nIntended enterprise end state. The intended end state is a single enterprise treatment using controlled emergency-access language, the canonical entity-code concept, the EAC-02 event-context fields, minimum-necessary identifier treatment, and retrospective review. This expresses the target control state without treating historical legacy artifacts as current proof of uniform conformity.\n6. Overall evidence position\nSupported by the frozen snapshot\nNot established by the frozen snapshot\nThe five labeled controls and their stated meanings; duplicate log representation; malformed or unresolved entity codes; inconsistent emergency-related labels; the need for event context, minimum-necessary identifiers, and retrospective review.\nThat every access labeled emergency, urgent, or break-glass met the emergency criterion, was properly authorized, or was compliant; a log label or a log row does not independently prove those matters.\nA recorded access event is distinct from the separate question whether it was necessary and justified as emergency access. Historical labels evidence what was recorded.\nThat an absent, malformed, or unresolved entity code proves unauthorized access or proves that no emergency existed; that a code or label defect decides the substantive access question.\nThe legacy arrangement remains during transition, and the intended enterprise treatment uses controlled language, canonical entity codes, event context, and review.\nUniversal compliance, universal non-compliance, a legal conclusion beyond the snapshot, a full historical reconciliation, patient-by-patient validation, or uniform target-state conformity in legacy records.\nConclusion. As of 30 June 2025, the frozen snapshot supports the stated control vocabulary, observed integrity and label issues, event-context requirement, minimum-necessary principle, and accountability review. It does not establish universal emergency status, authorization, compliance, non-compliance, or a broader legal conclusion. The standard therefore preserves the emergency boundary, the identity-central balance, and the transitional evidence limitation without converting imperfect historical records into conclusive findings.","truncated":false,"note":"DOCX 正文与表格单元格文字提取；不还原分页、字体与版式。","embedded_image_count":0,"embedded_images":[]}}],"rubric":[{"rubric_item_id":"R01","check":"file_introspection","category":"deterministic","criterion":"A file named revised_emergency_access_control_standard.docx is present at the required top-level location.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Submitted working-directory files.","grader_hint":"Check the exact filename and extension at the top level; do not award this for a renamed or nested file."},{"rubric_item_id":"R02","check":"execution","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The submitted DOCX opens successfully and its substantive text is extractable without corruption.","weight":1.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":true,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"DOCX package and rendered/extracted text.","grader_hint":"Open or render the document using standard offline office tooling and confirm that the document is readable."},{"rubric_item_id":"R03","check":"geometry","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The rendered document is no more than six pages.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered page count.","grader_hint":"Render with a standard office engine and count pages; do not infer page count from word count alone."},{"rubric_item_id":"R04","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document is practical for cross-functional approval and publication, with a clear title, coherent hierarchy, readable tables or lists where used, and no obvious drafting artifacts.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered document as a whole.","grader_hint":"Assess whether clinical, privacy, security, audit, and governance readers can navigate and reuse it without asking the drafter what a section means."},{"rubric_item_id":"R05","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies Wrenfield Health Network and identifies the document as a revised emergency-access control standard rather than a generic memo.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Title, scope, or opening statement.","grader_hint":"Look for the client identity and the nature of the instrument in visible document text."},{"rubric_item_id":"R06","check":"substring","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states the frozen snapshot's as-of date and makes clear that the evidence position is anchored to that date.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence basis, status, or applicability language.","grader_hint":"Compare the stated date with the as-of date in the supplied PDF; accept equivalent wording that makes the temporal boundary explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R07","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The scope covers enterprise emergency or break-glass access to patient or clinical records and the relevant workforce or support actors, without silently narrowing the standard to one department.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Purpose and scope provisions.","grader_hint":"The scope should fit the enterprise and cross-functional brief and should not omit the patient-record context."},{"rubric_item_id":"R08","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document identifies the frozen snapshot as its evidence basis and separates source observations from the standard's normative requirements.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence-status language and control-standard provisions.","grader_hint":"Award when the reader can tell what was observed in the snapshot versus what the revised standard requires."},{"rubric_item_id":"R09","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document expressly identifies the factual observations supported by the snapshot: duplicate access-log entries, malformed entity codes, and inconsistent emergency-related labels.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence summary or supported-facts section compared with the PDF.","grader_hint":"All three observations must be present and accurately described; do not award for a vague statement that the data are messy."},{"rubric_item_id":"R10","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document lists or otherwise accurately preserves all control labels in the snapshot that are presented as supported, without changing their meaning.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Control-label register or equivalent text compared with the supplied PDF.","grader_hint":"Compare every label and its described control meaning against the source; accept a clear table or integrated prose."},{"rubric_item_id":"R11","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not present a label such as emergency, urgent, or break-glass as independently proving that the underlying access satisfied the emergency criterion.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claims-status language and treatment of labels.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit limitation on the evidentiary force of labels, not merely an absence of the word 'proof'."},{"rubric_item_id":"R12","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document distinguishes a recorded access event from the separate question whether the event was justified as emergency access.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence interpretation and standard provisions.","grader_hint":"A log should establish that an event was recorded or occurred, but not by itself establish necessity, authorization, or compliance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R13","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that the snapshot does not establish that every access bearing an emergency-related label met the emergency criterion or was properly authorized.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Unsupported-claims or limitations section.","grader_hint":"Both the universal-validity and universal-authorization overclaims should be disclaimed or clearly limited."},{"rubric_item_id":"R14","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that an absent, malformed, or unresolved entity code does not by itself prove that access was unauthorized or that no emergency existed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entity-code interpretation and non-claims.","grader_hint":"The document must avoid converting an evidence-quality defect into a substantive conclusion."},{"rubric_item_id":"R15","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document defines emergency access by reference to an immediate patient-safety or continuity-of-care risk and the absence of a workable ordinary authorization path or a harmful delay from using it.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Emergency-access definition compared with EAC-01 in the PDF.","grader_hint":"The definition needs both the urgent risk and ordinary-approval-delay dimensions; generic 'urgent circumstances' is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R16","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document makes clear that break-glass access is not a convenient substitute for ordinary approval when the situation is planned, routine, or capable of tolerating the ordinary authorization path.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Prohibition or boundary provision.","grader_hint":"Check for an affirmative boundary against convenience use, not merely a repetition of the emergency definition."},{"rubric_item_id":"R17","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that a later label, explanation, or retrospective review cannot retroactively turn a non-emergency access into a genuine emergency.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Retrospective-rationalization limitation.","grader_hint":"The text should address retrospective rationalization directly and should not imply that paperwork cures the original absence of an emergency."},{"rubric_item_id":"R18","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The emergency boundary is expressed in a way that a cross-functional reader can apply to distinguish genuine emergencies from urgent-but-routine or convenience cases without relying on the inconsistent source labels.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Definitions, examples, or decision criteria in the document.","grader_hint":"Judge practical clarity and defensibility; the document need not use a particular heading or example."},{"rubric_item_id":"R19","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states the required justification and event context elements identified by the snapshot, including actor, event time, affected record or entity reference, reason, and outcome or review reference.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Justification or minimum-record provision compared with EAC-02 and EAC-03.","grader_hint":"Award only when the required elements are materially complete; a generic instruction to 'document the access' is not enough."},{"rubric_item_id":"R20","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document explains that a complete justification means enough contemporaneous context to assess necessity and link the event to the affected record, not a demand for an unrestricted clinical narrative.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Justification standard.","grader_hint":"Look for the distinction between evidentiary completeness and unnecessary narrative detail."},{"rubric_item_id":"R21","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document expressly addresses the edge case in which the patient's identity is central to understanding the emergency and states a deliberate trade-off between justification completeness and data minimization.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Edge-case provision.","grader_hint":"The document must name both competing requirements and make the chosen balancing principle explicit."},{"rubric_item_id":"R22","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"For the identity-central edge case, the document requires only the minimum patient identifier needed to establish the event's context and explains why that identifier is needed.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Privacy and justification provisions compared with DAT-02 in the PDF.","grader_hint":"Accept a defensible identifier formulation; require both minimization and a documented contextual rationale."},{"rubric_item_id":"R23","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that data minimization does not permit omitting information essential to identify or understand the emergency event.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Edge-case limitation or paired non-claim.","grader_hint":"The text should avoid treating minimization as an absolute instruction to remove all identifying context."},{"rubric_item_id":"R24","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that a complete justification does not authorize inclusion of extraneous patient identifiers or clinical detail unrelated to the access event.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Privacy limitation or paired non-claim.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit upper boundary on completeness, not only a general privacy aspiration."},{"rubric_item_id":"R25","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document avoids reproducing unnecessary patient-identifying or clinical detail from the source situation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered text and any examples.","grader_hint":"There is no need for names, diagnoses, addresses, or case-specific patient data; score down only if the document introduces such detail."},{"rubric_item_id":"R26","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document treats duplicate rows that represent the same underlying event as a log-integrity issue and does not count them as separate access events.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Audit-integrity provision compared with the duplicate-event relationship in the PDF.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent language such as one event with duplicate records; require that duplication not inflate the substantive event count."},{"rubric_item_id":"R27","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document preserves the distinction between duplicate records and genuinely distinct access events rather than collapsing all similar-looking rows.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Duplicate-handling language.","grader_hint":"The standard should not instruct readers to discard records merely because labels or timestamps resemble one another."},{"rubric_item_id":"R28","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that malformed or unresolved entity codes must remain visibly unresolved or be treated as an evidence limitation, rather than being silently repaired from an emergency label.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Entity-code and evidence-quality provision.","grader_hint":"Look for preservation of uncertainty and a ban on unsupported inference; a generic data-quality note is insufficient."},{"rubric_item_id":"R29","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document distinguishes the snapshot's canonical entity-code requirement from legacy or malformed codes and does not present the latter as conforming canonical identifiers.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Control-label or entity-taxonomy treatment compared with AUD-04 in the PDF.","grader_hint":"Check that the target code concept is not conflated with the historical defective values."},{"rubric_item_id":"R30","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document treats inconsistent emergency-related labels as a control-language or evidence-consistency problem and does not select one label as authoritative merely because it sounds strongest.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Label interpretation and evidence limitations.","grader_hint":"Award for an explicit non-inference from inconsistent labels; do not require a particular replacement taxonomy beyond the source-supported labels."},{"rubric_item_id":"R31","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document states that canonical labels and entity codes are controlled vocabulary or mapping requirements, while historical labels remain evidence of what was recorded rather than proof of target-state conformity.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition and taxonomy provisions.","grader_hint":"This criterion links the label problem to both evidentiary status and control-governance status."},{"rubric_item_id":"R32","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document includes retrospective review as a control requirement consistent with the snapshot.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Review or accountability provision compared with EAC-03.","grader_hint":"A post-event review requirement must be present, not merely a general statement that audits are useful."},{"rubric_item_id":"R33","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document pairs retrospective review with the limitation that review is not itself original authorization and does not cure an unjustified access.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Review limitation or paired non-claim.","grader_hint":"Look for both the rule and its doctrinal boundary in the same coherent framework."},{"rubric_item_id":"R34","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document avoids claiming that the snapshot proves universal compliance, universal non-compliance, or a legal conclusion beyond the evidence supplied.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Claims-status language and overall conclusions.","grader_hint":"The standard may identify control gaps or unsupported claims, but should not turn the frozen snapshot into an unsupported legal finding."},{"rubric_item_id":"R35","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document presents the legacy practice as transitional and explains how its historical records or labels relate to, but do not automatically satisfy, the enterprise target standard.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition-period provision.","grader_hint":"The transition discussion must preserve usability during change without treating legacy artifacts as target-state compliance."},{"rubric_item_id":"R36","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document describes the intended end state as a single enterprise treatment using the snapshot-supported controlled emergency-access language and canonical entity-code concept.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"End-state provision compared with the target-state material in the PDF.","grader_hint":"Accept equivalent wording, but the end state must be enterprise-wide and must address both labels and entity coding."},{"rubric_item_id":"R37","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not imply that the target-state standard is already fully in force in every legacy record or system.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transition wording.","grader_hint":"Look for an explicit or unmistakable distinction between current transitional conditions and the intended end state."},{"rubric_item_id":"R38","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document acknowledges that the 48-hour review window and unavailable full historical reconstruction constrain what can responsibly be concluded.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Evidence limitations, applicability, or transition language.","grader_hint":"The constraint must affect the scope of claims, not appear only as a copied deadline."},{"rubric_item_id":"R39","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document adopts an explicit, bounded, defensible fallback for the constrained review period that preserves uncertainty and avoids claiming more than the available evidence supports.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Transitional or evidence-limitation treatment.","grader_hint":"Two defensible approaches are acceptable: judge whether the chosen fallback is deliberate, bounded, internally coherent, and faithful to the source rather than looking for one prescribed workflow."},{"rubric_item_id":"R40","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document does not imply that the constrained review has completed a full historical reconciliation or patient-by-patient validation.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Limitations and conclusions.","grader_hint":"The document should state or clearly preserve the boundary of the available review."},{"rubric_item_id":"R41","check":"relational_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document pairs each major normative rule with an intelligible boundary on what the rule does not establish, especially for emergency status, labels, justification, privacy, and retrospective review.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rule and limitation language throughout the standard.","grader_hint":"Assess the doctrinal structure as a whole; a series of absolute commands without paired non-claims does not satisfy this item."},{"rubric_item_id":"R42","check":"constraint_predicate","category":"deterministic","criterion":"The document remains a standard and evidence statement and does not recommend an operational action, implementation plan, staffing choice, remediation program, or resource decision to Wrenfield Health Network or Marchbank Holdings.","weight":3.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Whole document, especially conclusion and transition sections.","grader_hint":"Control requirements and factual limitations are allowed; do not award if the conclusion tells the organizations what action to take."},{"rubric_item_id":"R43","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The document's conclusion gives a clear overall evidence position—what is supported and what is not established—without ending in a vague invitation to take future steps.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Conclusion or status summary.","grader_hint":"Judge whether a reviewer can identify the document's final doctrinal position without a further author explanation."},{"rubric_item_id":"R44","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"The language is precise, non-alarmist, and legally defensible: it uses calibrated terms such as supports, does not establish, unresolved, or not by itself where appropriate and avoids unsupported jurisdiction-specific assertions.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Rendered prose throughout.","grader_hint":"Assess professional legal drafting quality rather than searching for a single required phrase."},{"rubric_item_id":"R45","check":"judge","category":"semantic","criterion":"A clinical, privacy, security, audit, or governance reader can use the standard without asking the author to resolve the emergency boundary, evidence limitations, edge-case trade-off, or transition status.","weight":2.0,"polarity":"credit","scaffolding":false,"cross_deliverable":false,"required":false,"evidence":"Document as a reusable client artifact.","grader_hint":"This is an independent usability judgment: technically correct fragments that leave the competing criteria ambiguous should not receive credit."}],"spec":{"professional_context":"This is a doctrinal legal-control drafting assignment for an enterprise health-network client. The document must convert a frozen control snapshot and imperfect audit observations into a precise, reusable standard without overstating what the evidence proves.","difficulty_sources":["Distinguishing genuine emergency access from convenience use and retrospective rationalization when labels and entity codes are unreliable.","Expressing a complete-justification versus minimum-necessary-identifier trade-off without unnecessary sensitive detail.","Writing a doctrinal standard that is accurate during a legacy-to-enterprise transition and does not overclaim what a frozen snapshot proves.","Selecting and stating a bounded fallback under a 48-hour review constraint without prescribing an operational action."],"authoring_plan":{"reference_file":{"filename":"frozen_emergency_access_control_snapshot.pdf","format":"PDF","content":"Create one self-contained frozen external control snapshot with an explicit as-of date of 2025-06-30. It should identify the source as an external control-office snapshot applicable to the Wrenfield Health Network and Marchbank Holdings enterprise context, without containing real patient information.","control_vocabulary":[{"label":"EAC-01","meaning":"Emergency access is limited to an immediate patient-safety or continuity-of-care risk where ordinary authorization is unavailable or delay through it would create harmful delay."},{"label":"EAC-02","meaning":"The event record must contain the actor, event time, affected patient/record or entity reference, reason for access, and outcome or review reference sufficient to assess the event."},{"label":"EAC-03","meaning":"Emergency-access activity is subject to retrospective review; review is an accountability control and is not original authorization."},{"label":"DAT-02","meaning":"Audit and justification records use the minimum necessary patient identifier and related detail for the purpose of understanding and assessing the event."},{"label":"AUD-04","meaning":"Audit records use a canonical entity-code concept and preserve event integrity; malformed or unresolved codes are evidence limitations, not grounds for silent inference."}],"evidence_observations":["The frozen material contains duplicate log rows representing the same underlying access event; the source provides enough shared event attributes to distinguish duplicate representation from a distinct event.","Some entity-code values are malformed or cannot be resolved to the canonical entity-code concept.","Emergency-related labels are inconsistent across records, including emergency, urgent, and break-glass variants.","The observations support that the records and data-quality defects exist, but they do not establish that every emergency-related label denotes a justified emergency or that every such event was properly authorized.","A malformed or unresolved entity code does not establish that an access was unauthorized or that no emergency existed.","Historical labels remain evidence of what was recorded; they are not automatically conforming target-state labels."],"privacy_edge_case":"The snapshot recognizes that a complete justification can require a patient identity or record reference when identity is central to the emergency, while DAT-02 limits the record to the minimum identifier and context necessary. It does not support either an absolute rule to omit all identifiers or an unrestricted clinical narrative.","transition_context":"The source describes a legacy logging and label arrangement that remains during migration to one enterprise standard. The target state uses the supported emergency-access language, controlled labels, canonical entity-code treatment, event-context fields, and retrospective review. The source does not require the author to prescribe a particular implementation sequence.","claim_status":{"supported":["The named controls and their meanings in the control vocabulary.","The existence of duplicate log representation, malformed or unresolved entity codes, and inconsistent emergency-related labels.","The distinction between a recorded event and proof that the emergency criterion or authorization requirement was satisfied.","The need for event context, minimum necessary identifiers, and retrospective review."],"not_established":["That every record labeled emergency, urgent, or break-glass was a genuine emergency.","That every labeled event was authorized or compliant.","That a malformed code proves unauthorized access or proves that no emergency occurred.","That retrospective review cures an unjustified original access.","That minimization permits removal of context essential to understanding the event.","That completeness permits unrestricted patient detail.","That the target state already applies uniformly to all legacy records."]}},"gold_derivation":{"document_shape":"Build a concise, client-ready DOCX within six rendered pages. A strong reference solution should normally include a title/status line, purpose and scope, as-of/evidence basis, supported and unsupported claims, the doctrinal emergency-access standard, justification and privacy edge case, audit/data-quality treatment, transition/end-state treatment, and a concise conclusion. Do not require a specific page layout or heading names.","substantive_requirements":["Preserve all five source control labels and their meanings.","State the emergency criterion as immediate patient-safety or continuity-of-care risk plus unavailable or harmful-delay ordinary authorization.","Reject convenience use of break-glass access and retrospective rationalization.","Separate labels and log occurrence from proof of justification or authorization.","Treat duplicate representations as one underlying event when the source identifies them as such, while preserving the possibility of distinct events.","Do not silently repair malformed entity codes or use inconsistent labels as authoritative evidence.","State the EAC-02 event-context requirements.","Resolve the identity-central edge case through a deliberate minimum-necessary identifier trade-off with a reason for the identifier and a boundary against extraneous clinical detail.","Include retrospective review but state that it is not original authorization.","Explain the legacy transition and target enterprise end state without claiming full current conformity.","Acknowledge the 48-hour/no-full-reconstruction constraint and choose a bounded fallback that preserves uncertainty. The gold may use a provisional evidence-status treatment or another equally defensible approach.","End with a supported-versus-not-established evidence position, not an operational recommendation or next-step plan."],"quality_checks":["Render the DOCX offline and verify it is six pages or fewer.","Compare every control label and claim-status statement to the PDF.","Confirm no unsupported legal or jurisdiction-specific rule has been introduced.","Confirm no unnecessary patient-identifying detail appears.","Confirm the document is usable by the named cross-functional audience without author clarification."]}},"design_constraints":{"artifact_scope":"multi_artifact_bundle","structure":"required_sections"},"reference_files":[{"artifact_id":"frozen_emergency_access_control_snapshot","filename":"frozen_emergency_access_control_snapshot.pdf","purpose":"The sole frozen external evidence source for the emergency-access control labels, supported and unsupported claims, audit observations, privacy requirement, and transition context used in the revised standard.","required":true,"discovery":"Open this PDF from the supplied reference files; no other external source is needed or permitted."}],"deliverables":[{"deliverable_id":"revised_emergency_access_control_standard","filename":"revised_emergency_access_control_standard.docx","media_type":"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.wordprocessingml.document","description":"A client-ready revised emergency-access control standard for Wrenfield Health Network and the Marchbank Holdings transition, grounded in the supplied frozen snapshot.","required":true,"constraints":["Use exactly this filename and write the file to the top level of the working directory.","The rendered document must not exceed six pages.","State the snapshot as-of date and distinguish supported evidence from claims the evidence cannot establish.","Address the emergency-versus-convenience distinction, the justification and data-minimization edge case, duplicate and malformed log evidence, and both the transition period and intended end state.","Do not include an operational recommendation, implementation plan, staffing or resource recommendation, or patient-identifying detail not needed for the standard."],"rendering_required":true}],"work_activity":"evaluate_compliance","work_product":"legal_policy_document","internet_policy":"forbidden","evidence_policy":"professional_judgment"},"source_metadata":{"prompt_version":"authored_doc@v4","occupation":"lawyers","sector":"professional_scientific_technical_services","task_archetype":"authored_doc","estimated_professional_hours":4.0,"gold_policy_self_report_not_verified":{"author_type":"hybrid","reviewer_policy":"Have an independent document-quality reviewer verify page count, file usability, and absence of operational recommendations, and have a domain reviewer compare every supported label, claim limitation, emergency boundary, privacy trade-off, duplicate treatment, and transition statement with the frozen snapshot and occupational expectations. Accept either deliberate defensible fallback for the constrained review window.","minimum_reviewers":2,"known_limitations":["The exact visual layout may vary across offline DOCX renderers, so page-count review should use a standard rendering path and focus on the rendered result.","The source intentionally supports doctrinal judgment rather than a single mandatory prose template; equivalent precise formulations should receive credit."],"reference_solution_passed_rubric":true}},"validation":{"label":"成品包存在","detail":"按公开题面存在纳入抽样；不视为内容正确性验收。","formal":false,"release_ready_recorded":true,"checks":[{"check":"package_integrity","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T14:49:33+00:00"},{"check":"gold_isolation","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T14:49:33+00:00"},{"check":"artifact_format","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T14:49:33+00:00"},{"check":"rendering","validator":"gdpval-package","validator_version":"v1","passed":true,"required":true,"metrics":{},"evidence_artifact_ids":[],"detail":"","checked_at":"2026-08-16T14:49:33+00:00"}]},"source_path":"/inspire/hdd2/project/special-project-1/public/ynan/gdpval_data/corpus_main/runs/9b916f31a7713596","declared_deliverables":["revised_emergency_access_control_standard.docx"]};
